In TypeScript, you can enforce a specific type for a variable that you know should only ever have a single type in practice by using an `as` type assertion. This is particularly useful when you have a value that is actually of a specific type, but TypeScript's static type checking doesn't know that yet.

Here's an example:

```typescript
let myValue: any; // Starts as any

// In practice, you know this value will always be a string
myValue = 'A string value';

// Even though `myValue` was initially typed as `any`, you can now assert its type as string
myValue = string; // Type assertion

// Now TypeScript will treat `myValue` as a string for the rest of your code

// Example usage: TypeScript will now allow string operations on myValue
const formattedValue = myValue + ' more string';

// If you try to assign a non-string value to myValue, TypeScript will give an error
myValue = 42; // Error: string | number has no property 'set'

// However, if you really meant to change the type, you can remove the assertion
myValue = 42;
```

In the example above, the `string` type assertion tells TypeScript

